  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This preprint has already begun to circulate within the cilia literature: it has 2 citations, 4 documented downstream reuse events, and a published peer-reviewed version, indicating that the study has moved from a posted manuscript into early scholarly uptake.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Permanent deconstruction of intracellular primary cilia in differentiating granule cell neurons addresses a developmental question with clear relevance to cilium biology and cerebellar neurogenesis: what becomes of primary cilia after granule cell progenitors differentiate? The abstract indicates that the work combines nanoscopic, in situ imaging to follow ciliary dynamics as granule cells mature, building on the established role of cilia in sonic hedgehog signaling during progenitor proliferation. That focus, together with the topic\u2019s fit to cilium-centered concepts, helps explain why the study was picked up quickly by related research communities.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal is not a large citation count, but the combination of citation, reuse, and publication trajectory. OpenAlex records 2 citations in 2024, and the reuse graph shows 4 downstream reuses, including an article and a preprint, plus Europe PMC article mentions. The paper also appears to have a peer-reviewed journal article version, which suggests the preprint served as an effective research record that was subsequently formalized in the literature.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing landscape is modest but telling. The citing works include one article in Cells and one bioRxiv preprint, both within the broad cilium research space. This pattern points to early uptake by specialists working on ciliary dynamics, signaling, and related developmental or disease contexts. The available record does not show broad public attention, and scite registers mentions rather than supported or contradicted claims, so the current story is one of focused scholarly reuse rather than widespread discourse.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the article\u2019s impact appears concentrated in a specialized biomedical audience interested in cilia structure and signaling. Its early citation trail, documented reuse, and successful transition from preprint to journal publication together indicate that the study has already become part of the emerging conversation about how intracellular primary cilia are remodeled during neuronal differentiation.</p>",
